                    should_clone_vardef = true
                  }
                  if (((vpLN.type_name == "buffer") || (vpLN.type_name == "int_buffer")) || (vpLN.type_name == "double_buffer")) {
                    should_clone_vardef = true
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
          if should_clone_vardef {
            ; a str reference (static string, borrowed &str parameter) makes
            ; an owned String with .to_string(); .clone() would copy the &str
            if (vardef_src_ref_str == false) {
              if (((value.expression == false) && value.hasParamDesc) && ((array_length value.ns) == 1)) {
                def vbp:RangerAppParamDesc value.paramDesc
                if (vbp.rust_borrow_type == 1) {
                  def vbpNN@(optional):CodeNode vbp.nameNode
                  if (!null? vbpNN) {
                    def vbpN:CodeNode (unwrap vbpNN)
                    if ((vbpN.type_name == "string") && ((strlen vbpN.array_type) == 0)) {
                      vardef_src_ref_str = true
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
            if vardef_src_ref_str {
              wr.out(".to_string()" false)
            } {
              ; a borrowed collection or buffer parameter is a SLICE, and
              ; cloning one gives another slice — the owned slot wants a Vec
              if (this.rustSliceRefRead(value)) {
                wr.out(".to_vec()" false)
              } {
                wr.out(".clone()" false)
              }
            }
          }
        }
      } {
        if (nn.value_type == RangerNodeType.Array) {
          wr.out(" = Vec::new()" false)
        }
        if (nn.value_type == RangerNodeType.Hash) {
          wr.out(" = HashMap::default()" false)
        }
        ; Initialize optional variables to None in Rust
        if (nameN.hasFlag("optional")) {
          wr.out(" = None" false)
        }
      }
      if (initMark >= 0) {
        wr.stripLineMarkParens(initMark)
      }
      wr.out(";" false)
      if ((p.ref_cnt == 0) && (p.is_class_variable == true)) {
        wr.out("     // note: unused" false)
      }
      if ((p.ref_cnt == 0) && (p.is_class_variable == false)) {
        wr.newline()
      } {
        wr.newline()
      }
    }
  }
  ; The constructor of a `record` holds a marker parameter for each keyword,
  ; next to the parameter that carries the value. The marker has a name and no
  ; type, and Rust has no keyword argument, so the marker must not reach the
  ; signature: without this filter `record Point { def xpos:int 0 }` comes out
  ; as `pub fn new(xpos : , xpos : i64) -> Point`, which does not parse.
  ; PLAN_RUST_OWNERSHIP 2b support: is the named class one the sharing
  ; analysis marked? On by default; -rust-value-classes opts out.
  ; D-UNION: is this expression a `new` of a class that lives in a cell? A
  ; value going into a union-typed slot (Rc<dyn Any>) has to carry its own
  ; cell; a local or a field already holds one, a fresh construction does not,
  ; so the construction site is where the wrap has to go.
  ; Is `memberName` one of this union's members?
  ; The declared class of a value node, as the source named it: a name carries
  ; its desc, a `new` carries the class it constructs. Empty when neither.
  ; Writes a value into a union-typed slot (PLAN_SHAPES.md S5) and answers true,
  ; or answers false and writes nothing when the normal path should handle it.
  ; A union is a Rust enum, so a MEMBER value has to be wrapped in its variant —
  ; at the construction site if it is a `new`, around the name otherwise — while
  ; a value that is already the union passes as a clone of the handle.
  ; The name of the sealable union a function returns, or "" when it returns
  ; anything else.
  ; A case the enum carries BY VALUE (a member of a sealable union that is not
  ; shared). Its identity question has no object behind it, so the generated
  ; RgIdentical arm compares content — which needs PartialEq on the struct.
  ; Would rustWriteUnionValue have to WRAP this value in an enum variant to
  ; put it into a slot of `targetTypeName`? True only for a member of a
  ; sealable union; a value already of the family needs no wrap, and neither
  ; does anything else. Asked ahead of writing, so a caller can commit to a
  ; prefix (`&`) knowing the wrap will follow.
  ; A method of a shared class that uses `this` as a value needs the Rc that
  ; holds the receiver, and `&mut self` cannot reach it — the shared_from_this
  ; question in Rust form. Such a method takes a hidden first parameter
  ; `__self_rc: &Rc<RefCell<T>>`, and every call site passes the receiver's
  ; Rc. The reference is safe next to the `&mut self` borrow because the
  ; callee only clones or downgrades the Rc; it never borrows the cell again.
  ; A lambda body is written inside the body of the method that declares it,
  ; so the `__self_rc` in scope there is the *enclosing* method's hidden
  ; argument. The lambda's own desc never carries the flag —
  ; computeSelfRcNeeds walks `cl.methods` and skips lambdas — so asking the
  ; lambda whether it holds the Rc always answers no, and a `this.method(…)`
  ; inside a `forEach` body would be reported as an impossible call from a
  ; constructor. Hop through `insideFn` to the real method before asking.
  ; How a def initializer relates to Rc<RefCell<T>> when the local is a
  ; shared-class name: 0 = a raw value that needs the wrap, 1 = another
  ; wrapped name (share it by cloning the Rc), 2 = an expression that already
  ; produces an owned Rc (the upgrade of a weak field).
  ; Write the receiver's Rc as the hidden first argument of a call to a
  ; method that needs it. True when something was written.
  ; The type the hidden receiver argument is declared with. A method of a
  ; TRAIT interface is declared once and implemented by every subclass, and
  ; each impl forwards to its own inherent method — so the hidden argument
  ; cannot be typed as the container class or the two disagree
  ; (`&Rc<RefCell<Parent>>` against `&Rc<RefCell<Child>>`). The trait object
  ; is the one type all of them share.
  ; …and the handle a call site holds is the CONCRETE class wherever the
  ; receiver was declared as one. Rust will not pass `&Rc<RefCell<Child>>`
  ; where `&Rc<RefCell<dyn ParentTrait>>` is declared, so the unsizing goes in
  ; here. An empty answer means the two already agree.
  ; the receiver of a method signature; the trailing comma is written by
  ; writeArgsDef, and only when something follows
  ; The argument list a trait forwarding body hands on to the inherent
  ; method. It has to mirror writeArgsDef exactly — the same names, the same
  ; skipped keyword parameters, and the hidden __self_rc where the method
  ; takes one — or the forwarding call does not match the thing it calls.
  fn writeArgsDef (fnDesc:RangerAppFunctionDesc ctx:RangerAppWriterContext wr:CodeWriter) {
    def pms (filter (fnDesc.params) {
      if (item.nameNode.hasFlag("keyword")) {
        return false
      }
      return true
    })
    def lead_comma:boolean rust_receiver_written
    rust_receiver_written = false
    def wrote_selfrc:boolean false
    ; In a re-entrant trait family the `&self` receiver IS the handle, so the
    ; signature must not declare a second one.
    ; docs/plans/PLAN_RUST_REENTRANCY.md.
    if ((this.rustNeedsSelfRc(fnDesc ctx)) && (rust_trait_self_is_handle == false)) {
      if lead_comma {
        wr.out(", " false)
      }
      def sccName:string (this.rustSelfRcParamType(fnDesc ctx))
      wr.out((("__self_rc: &Rc<RefCell<" + sccName) + ">>") false)
      wrote_selfrc = true
    }
    for pms arg:RangerAppParamDesc i {
      if (((i > 0) || wrote_selfrc) || lead_comma) {
        wr.out(", " false)
      }
      def nameN:CodeNode (unwrap arg.nameNode)
      ; In Rust, object parameters that might have fields modified need 'mut'
      def v_type:RangerNodeType nameN.value_type
      if ((v_type == RangerNodeType.Object) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.VRef) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.NoType)) {
        v_type = (nameN.typeNameAsType(ctx))
      }
      def is_object:boolean ((v_type == RangerNodeType.Object) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.Array) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.Hash) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.IntBuffer) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.DoubleBuffer) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.CharBuffer) || (v_type == RangerNodeType.Buffer))
      ; Use compiledName to include any name transformations (e.g. len -> __len)
      def paramName:string (this.adjustType(arg.compiledName))
      ; a `mut` BINDING is needed only when the body reassigns the parameter
      ; itself. `fn show(mut who: &mut User)` writes through the reference,
      ; which the `&mut` covers; the binding never moves, and the `mut` was
      ; rustc's unused_mut on every such signature.
      def rust_mut_pfx:string ""
      if (arg.set_cnt > 0) {
        rust_mut_pfx = "mut "
      }
      ; …and a parameter that arrives BY mutable reference keeps it: the body
      ; may pass it on as `&mut name`, which reborrows through the binding
      ; and so needs the binding mutable. Dropping it there was measured at
      ; 11 rustc errors, every one an E0596 on a `&mut` parameter handed to
      ; another call. Whether this particular one is passed on is not
      ; answerable from a signature, so the header is asked instead.
      if (rust_mut_pfx == "") {
        if ((arg.rust_borrow_type == 2) || arg.needs_cpp_reference) {
          rust_mut_pfx = "mut "
          this.rustNeedAllow("unused_mut" wr)
        }
      }
      if rust_in_trait_decl {
        rust_mut_pfx = ""
      }
      
      ; Check static analysis flags for mutable reference requirement
      ; rust_borrow_type: 0=owned, 1=borrow, 2=mut_borrow
      ; needs_cpp_reference also indicates mutation (same analysis)
      def needsMutRef:boolean false
      if arg.needs_cpp_reference {
        needsMutRef = true
      }
      if (arg.rust_borrow_type == 2) {
        needsMutRef = true
      }
      
      ; Check if parameter is assigned to a weak field - needs Rc<RefCell<T>> type
      ; This is detected by static analysis when we see: this.weakField = param
      if arg.rust_needs_rc_wrap {
        ; A proven-borrowed Rc parameter takes a reference: the callee reads the
        ; handle and never stores it, so every call site passes `&x` instead of
        ; cloning. `.borrow()` / `.borrow_mut()` work the same through it.
        ; a plain class name is the whole shared type on its own — a class
        ; with subclasses already reads as Rc<RefCell<dyn XTrait>>, so
        ; wrapping writeTypeDef here would give it a second cell
        def argIsPlainClass:boolean (((strlen nameN.array_type) == 0) && ((strlen nameN.key_type) == 0))
        if (argIsPlainClass && ((strlen nameN.type_name) == 0)) {
          argIsPlainClass = false
        }
        if (arg.rust_borrow_type == 1) {
          if argIsPlainClass {
            wr.out(((paramName + ": &") + (this.rustSharedTypeString(nameN.type_name ctx))) false)
          } {
            wr.out((paramName + ": &Rc<RefCell<") false)
            this.writeTypeDef(nameN ctx wr)
            wr.out(">>" false)
          }
          continue
        }
        ; Parameter is assigned to weak field - needs Rc<RefCell<T>> type
        if argIsPlainClass {
          ; an @(optional) parameter is Option<…>: the body asks it
          ; `is_none()` and unwraps it, and a caller may have nothing to pass
          if (nameN.hasFlag("optional")) {
            wr.out((((((rust_mut_pfx + paramName) + ": Option<") + (this.rustSharedTypeString(nameN.type_name ctx))) + ">")) false)
          } {
            wr.out((((rust_mut_pfx + paramName) + ": ") + (this.rustSharedTypeString(nameN.type_name ctx))) false)
          }
        } {
          wr.out((rust_mut_pfx + paramName + ": Rc<RefCell<") false)
          this.writeTypeDef(nameN ctx wr)
          wr.out(">>" false)
        }
        continue
      }
      
      ; Check for immutable borrow (rust_borrow_type = 1)
      ; This is for buffer/array/object params that are not mutated
      def needsImmutableBorrow:boolean (arg.rust_borrow_type == 1)
      
      ; Check if parameter is assigned - if so, make it mut
      if needsMutRef {
        ; Parameter is mutated - needs mut
        wr.out((rust_mut_pfx + paramName + ": &mut ") false)
        this.writeTypeDef(nameN ctx wr)
      } {
        if needsImmutableBorrow {
          ; Parameter is borrowed but not mutated - immutable reference.
          ; A borrowed collection or buffer of scalars is a slice: clippy's
          ; ptr_arg — `&Vec<u8>` involves a new object where `&[u8]` will
          ; do, and `&Vec` coerces to `&[T]` at every call site.
          def slice_elem:string ""
          ; a HASH carries its value type in array_type as well, so the
          ; key_type has to be empty for this to be an array — a
          ; `[string:string]` parameter came out as `&[String]` and every
          ; map call on it was a missing method
          if (((strlen nameN.array_type) > 0) && ((strlen nameN.key_type) == 0)) {
            def ael:string nameN.array_type
            if (((((ael == "int") || (ael == "double")) || (ael == "boolean")) || (ael == "string")) || (ael == "char")) {
              slice_elem = (this.getObjectTypeString(ael ctx))
            }
          } {
            if (nameN.type_name == "buffer") {
              slice_elem = "u8"
            }
            if (nameN.type_name == "int_buffer") {
              slice_elem = "i64"
            }
            if (nameN.type_name == "double_buffer") {
              slice_elem = "f64"
            }
          }
          if ((strlen slice_elem) > 0) {
            wr.out((((paramName + ": &[") + slice_elem) + "]") false)
          } {
            ; a borrowed read-only string is &str: every call site coerces
            ; (&String and &'static str alike), no allocation either way
            if (((nameN.type_name == "string") && ((strlen nameN.array_type) == 0)) && ((strlen nameN.key_type) == 0)) {
              wr.out((paramName + ": &str") false)
            } {
              wr.out((paramName + ": &") false)
              this.writeTypeDef(nameN ctx wr)
            }
          }
        } {
          ; writeTypeDef reads the `optional` flag off the name node and writes
          ; the `Option<…>` itself. Wrapping it again here gave every
          ; `@(optional)` PARAMETER the type `Option<Option<T>>` — for a scalar,
          ; a string and an object alike — which no call site and no `unwrap` in
          ; the body agrees with. An optional LOCAL and an optional RETURN never
          ; went through here, which is why only parameters were wrong.
          if is_object {
            wr.out((rust_mut_pfx + paramName + ": ") false)
          } {
            if (arg.set_cnt > 0) {
              wr.out((rust_mut_pfx + paramName + ": ") false)
            } {
              wr.out((paramName + ": ") false)
            }
          }
          this.writeTypeDef(nameN ctx wr)
        }
      }
    }
  }

  ; Override CreateCallExpression to handle optional types in Rust
  ; When calling a method on an optional object, we need to unwrap it first
  ; Helper: Check if a node tree contains any self (this) reference
  ; Any function or method call anywhere in the tree. Used to decide
  ; whether an argument can re-enter the object graph while the
  ; receiver's RefCell borrow is held.
  ; Helper: Check if a function body uses 'this' (self) - for Rust static method optimization
  ; Methods that don't reference 'this' or class variables can be emitted as static methods
  ; This avoids E0499 errors when calling such methods with &mut self.field arguments
  ; Does this second segment of a `this.…` path name a METHOD of the current
  ; class rather than one of its fields?
  ; Does the body need the STRUCT — a field of this object — as opposed to
  ; reaching the object through its handle? A bare `this` is written as the
  ; hidden __self_rc argument, and `this.method(…)` can be called through the
  ; same handle, borrowing for that call and no longer. A field read or write
  ; is what actually needs a receiver.
  ; The receiver question for one method: does it need a `self` parameter?
  ; A method that reaches itself only THROUGH its handle does not — see
  ; fnBodyUsesThisStruct — but only when it actually carries the handle.
  ; Does this body call a method on `this` that hands back another handle of
  ; the same class? Such a handle may BE this object.
  ; Helper: Check if a node accesses a field of the given variable
  ; Returns true if node is like varName.field or varName.field.subfield
  ; Helper: Get root variable name from an argument node
  ; For a simple var reference like "child", returns "child"
  ; Helper: Detect when we have a &mut ref conflict in function call arguments
  ; Returns true if argIdx gets &mut and other args access fields of that same variable
  ; ====== CALL GRAPH ANALYSIS FOR MUTATION DETECTION ======
  ; These functions analyze method bodies to determine if they mutate 'this' (self)
  ; We build a call graph to handle transitive mutations properly
  
  ; Helper: Collect all self-method calls from a node (returns method names)
  ; `reader.seek(x)` — a method called on a field of THIS object, where the
  ; field holds another object inline. The callee borrows the field, the
  ; field is reached through `self`, and so a callee that takes `&mut self`
  ; makes the method around it `&mut self` too.
  ;
  ; The `has_call` rule further down asks the same question, but only of the
  ; `(call obj method args)` node the front end builds for a call on an
  ; EXPRESSION. An ordinary `reader.seek(x)` is a `hasFnCall` node whose
  ; receiver lives inside the dotted name `reader.seek`, and nothing looked
  ; there — which is what 277 of the 396 rustc errors on the PPTX viewer
  ; were, one bug in 267 different functions.
  ; The same question of a call receiver PATH alone: `reader.seek` or
  ; `this.reader.seek`. The emission asks it too — a path head that is
  ; `this` has to take the mutable borrow when the field it reaches is held
  ; inline and the callee writes to it.
  ; Is this node a value reached through `self` — `this`, a field of this
  ; object, or a path that starts at one? A reference taken to any of them
  ; is a reference into this struct.
  ; `Helper.touch(own)` where the callee takes that parameter by `&mut`.
  ; The argument is written `&mut self.own`, which borrows this struct
  ; mutably — so does `this` itself passed the same way, which comes out
  ; `&mut self`. Either makes the method around the call `&mut self`.
  ; Helper: Check if method body DIRECTLY mutates this (field assignments, optional field access)
  ; Does NOT consider transitive mutations via method calls
  ; is this call receiver a plain collection, string or buffer — a value
  ; whose operators only write through self when the operator itself is a
  ; mutating one? A user-class receiver never qualifies: any of its methods
  ; could mutate it, and a user method may shadow an operator name.
  ; the operator names whose first operand is written through — the same
  ; list the template engine flags as LHS targets
  ; Build call graph and mutation info for a class
  ; Returns two maps: methodName -> directlyMutates (boolean), methodName -> MethodCallList
  ; The same walk over the ancestors, for the methods a class inherits. They
  ; are emitted into this class's impl and can be called from its own
  ; methods, so leaving them out of the graph made a caller of an inherited
  ; mutating method look immutable — `&self` calling a `&mut self` method.
  ; A name the class defines itself is already in the maps and wins.
  ; A method of the trait interface is emitted `&mut self` whatever its body
  ; does — the same rule the signature writer applies — so anything that
  ; calls one needs a mutable receiver too. buildInheritedMutationGraph
  ; already says this for methods only the parent declares; a class that
  ; OVERRIDES a trait method had its own (possibly non-mutating) answer
  ; recorded first, and the override is emitted `&mut self` all the same.
  ; A trait method and the inherent method each impl forwards to share one
  ; signature, and the forwarding body is written from the PARENT's variant.
  ; Either side wanting the hidden __self_rc argument therefore commits both
  ; to it, or the forwarding call is an argument off in one direction or the
  ; other. These are the descriptors the writer emits from — cl.methods holds
  ; different ones, which is why aligning through those changed nothing.
  ; The root of the trait family a class belongs to: itself when it has
  ; children, otherwise the parent that does.
  ; Does this call receiver reach through a field that holds an Rc? Such a
  ; call borrows the field's cell, not this object's struct.
  ; Can this field live behind a Cell? A scalar only: a collection is mutated
  ; in place through the field, a shared class already has a cell of its own,
  ; and an Option needs the field itself to be reseated.
  ; A Copy scalar goes in a Cell, which never borrows at all. A String has to
  ; use a RefCell, and the borrow lasts one statement.
  ; a String cell owns its value on the way in; every other RefCell takes the
  ; value as it stands
  ; a collection cell is borrowed rather than copied out
  ; Does this node name a cell field — as a bare name, as `this.f`, or at the
  ; end of a path through another object?
  fn rustNodeIsCellField:boolean (node:CodeNode) {
    def cfD@(optional):RangerAppParamDesc (this.rustCellFieldDesc(node))
    return (!null? cfD)
  }

  ; Every scalar field of a class in a trait family that the trait does not
  ; expose through an accessor. The root's own fields are left alone: their
  ; accessors hand out references, which a cell cannot serve.
  ; One answer per trait method for the whole family, computed once and cached
  ; on the root. A trait method is `&mut self` if ANY implementation of it
  ; mutates its own object — the declaration and every impl share one
  ; signature, so the answer has to be the union. It used to be a blanket
  ; `true`: every method of every writer took `&mut self`, which means every
  ; call through a writer handle takes the cell for the whole call, and the
  ; compiler's writers call back into each other constantly.
  ; Check if a method transitively mutates (directly or via called methods)
  ; Uses visited set to prevent infinite recursion on cycles
  ; Main entry: Check if a method mutates this (uses call graph for transitive analysis)
  ; Legacy function - kept for compatibility but uses new analysis
  ; Does `<obj>.<methodName>(…)` land on a method that takes the hidden
  ; __self_rc argument, when the front end left node.fnDesc unresolved?
  ; Resolves through the receiver's evaluated type (the current class for a
  ; `this` receiver).
  ; True when an argument is a named local/param — or a dotted field read,
  ; which comes out of a Ref and cannot move (E0507) — whose type moves on
  ; a by-value pass (String, collection, object): the call would consume
  ; the value, so the call site has to clone. Copy scalars, weak refs and
  ; optionals stay untouched.
  ; Override CreateMethodCall for Rust to handle borrow conflicts with optional self members
  ; This is used for is_direct_method_call nodes (e.g., streamBuffer.writeString(...))
  ; Check if a node is a self method call (this.method(...))
  ; The self calls an argument only CONTAINS, rather than IS. isSelfMethodCall
  ; tests the argument's own head, so a call reached through anything else —
  ; most often an index — was never seen: `itemAt outR (this.nearestIndex(…))`
  ; borrows self to compute the index while the enclosing call is borrowing it
  ; too, which is E0499. rustc's own advice is a local per call, so they are
  ; collected here for the caller to hoist.
  ; Each is collected AS IT SITS in the tree, parens and all: the operand and
  ; index writers look for rust_use_tmpvar on the child they hold, before any
  ; unwrapping of their own.
  ; Nothing that opens a SCOPE is searched, and the search stops at the branch
  ; rather than stepping over it. A loop body is the case that matters: `for
  ; xs item { this.markAsyncFrom(item visited) }` becomes a closure taking
  ; `item`, so the call in it borrows self when the closure runs, not here, and
  ; a `let` lifted out of it lands where `item` does not exist. That is a block
  ; node, not a lambda_ctx, so both are tested — along with a subtree that
  ; merely CONTAINS a closure, which is skipped whole: an argument this pass
  ; leaves alone still compiles, one hoisted out of its scope does not.
  ; Check if any argument contains a self method call - returns the argument index, -1 if none
  ; Static array literals -- ([] a b c) / ([] _:T (a b c)).
  ;
  ; Without this override Rust inherits the generic writer, which emits a
  ; fixed-size array `[a, b, c]` of type [T; N]. Every Ranger array is a Vec<T>,
  ; so the result never compiled: "expected `Vec<K>`, found `[K; 3]`". Rust
  ; spells the Vec form `vec![...]`, which is also what the surrounding
  ; push-based code lowers to, so the two spellings now agree.
  fn writeArrayLiteral( node:CodeNode ctx:RangerAppWriterContext wr:CodeWriter) {
    wr.out("vec![" false)
    node.children.forEach({
      if( index > 0 ) {
        wr.out(", " false)
      }
      ; an element is an EXPRESSION — without saying so, a call inside one
      ; still reads as a statement, and anything hoisted before it lands in
      ; the middle of the literal
      ctx.setInExpr()
      this.WalkNode( item ctx wr )
      ctx.unsetInExpr()
      ; a literal element is OWNED by the Vec, so a NAME read into one moves
      ; unless it is cloned — and the name goes on being used after the
      ; literal, which is what Ranger's semantics say. A Copy scalar needs
      ; nothing (clippy's clone_on_copy), and a borrowed handle clones too.
      def alCloned:boolean false
      if (this.rustArgIsNameRead(item)) {
        def alCopy:boolean false
        if item.hasParamDesc {
          def alP:RangerAppParamDesc (unwrap item.paramDesc)
          alCopy = (this.rustFieldIsCopyScalar(alP ctx))
        } {
          alCopy = true
        }
        if (alCopy == false) {
          if (this.rustStrRefRead(item)) {
            wr.out(".to_string()" false)
          } {
            wr.out(".clone()" false)
          }
          alCloned = true
        }
      }
      if (alCloned == false) {
        if (this.rustValueIsBorrowedHandle(item ctx)) {
          wr.out(".clone()" false)
        }
      }
    })
    wr.out("]" false)
  }
  ; The Rust body of the synthetic `__singleton` accessor the resolver
  ; advertises for a @singleton(true) class (findStaticMethod builds the desc;
  ; call sites already emit `ClassName::__singleton()`). One instance per
  ; thread in a lazy slot: singletons are shared classes, so the accessor
  ; hands out the Rc and first-call constructor arguments win, matching es6.
  ; does any class of the program declare a `sfn m@(main)` entry?
  ; Custom operator handler for Rust - handles push with string conversion and optional assignments
  ; the rust templates of these operators already write a full `( … )`
  ; (bit ops, array_length) or an atomic postfix form (itemAt, format!) —
  ; the expression walker's extra pair around them is clippy's
  ; double_parens
  ; one operand of an operator custom: bare when atomic (a literal, a
  ; name, a path), `( … )` otherwise — and the child is told its caller
  ; delimits it, so it adds no pair of its own
  ; is this argument already a `&` reference — a borrowed parameter passed
  ; straight through? `f(&x)` where x is `&[u8]` is clippy's
  ; needless_borrow; the name alone re-borrows correctly.
  ; the &mut twin: a mut-reference parameter passed straight through needs
  ; no second `&mut` — the name reborrows
  ; a bare `this` handed to a `&mut T` parameter. `self` is already the
  ; reference the parameter wants, so `&mut self` takes a reference TO the
  ; binding — and that binding is not declared `mut`, which is an error in
  ; a `&self` and a `&mut self` method alike. `&mut *self` reborrows it.
  ; A `&mut` argument that is a plain dotted path — `sc.zone`, `pres.theme`.
  ; Nothing is called inside it, so marking the walk cannot reach a nested
  ; call's own receiver.
  ; The borrow an argument takes for a `&mut T` parameter.
  ; one side of a string comparison: a literal stays `"lit"` — String
  ; compares with &str directly
  ; an operand cast to f64: an integer literal is the f64 literal itself
  ; --- string concatenation as format! (PLAN_RUST_IDIOMATICITY item 5) ---

  ; unwraps `( x )` nesting to the single meaningful child
  ; A closure literal has to stay in the call that takes it. Rust infers a
  ; closure's parameter types from the `&mut dyn FnMut(…)` it is passed to,
  ; and a `let __arg_0 = &mut |item| …;` ahead of the call has no expected
  ; type at all — "type annotations needed" on every parameter.
  ; Does this expression already produce an Option? A call whose return is
  ; declared `@(optional)` does, and so does a read of an optional name.
  ; Wrapping one in Some() gives an Option holding an Option.
  fn rustExprIsOptional:boolean (inNode:CodeNode ctx:RangerAppWriterContext) {
    ; the expression is usually parenthesised — `x = (ctx.findVariable(n))` —
    ; and the wrapper carries neither the flag nor the call's descriptor
    def node:CodeNode (this.rustUnwrapParens(inNode))
    if (node.hasFlag("optional")) {
      return true
    }
    if (inNode.hasFlag("optional")) {
      return true
    }
    if (!null? node.fnDesc) {
      def fd:RangerAppFunctionDesc (unwrap node.fnDesc)
      def fdNN@(optional):CodeNode fd.nameNode
      if (!null? fdNN) {
        if ((unwrap fdNN).hasFlag("optional")) {
          return true
        }
      }
    }
    return false
  }

  fn rustNodeIsLambda:boolean (node:CodeNode) {
    def real:CodeNode (this.rustUnwrapParens(node))
    if (!null? real.lambda_ctx) {
      return true
    }
    return false
  }

  ; `(itemAt runs ri).color` — a field read on an EXPRESSION rather than on
  ; a name. The generic writer puts the object in parentheses and writes the
  ; field after it; on Rust an expression that yields a shared class yields
  ; an `Rc<RefCell<T>>`, which has no fields of its own, so the read has to
  ; go through the cell first ("no field `color` on type `Rc<RefCell<…>>`").
  fn CreatePropertyGet:void (node:CodeNode ctx:RangerAppWriterContext wr:CodeWriter) {
    def obj:CodeNode (node.getSecond())
    def prop:CodeNode (node.getThird())
    this.writeCallReceiver(obj ctx wr)
    def pgType:string obj.eval_type_name
    if ((strlen pgType) == 0) {
      pgType = obj.type_name
    }
    ; …but a CALL already hands back a borrow of its own result, and a
    ; second one is `.borrow().borrow()`
    def pgInner:CodeNode (this.rustUnwrapParens(obj))
    if (pgInner.hasFnCall || pgInner.has_call) {
      pgType = ""
    }
    ; …and so does the PROPERTY, when the front end resolved which class it
    ; belongs to — WriteVRef writes the borrow in front of a `.name` whose
    ; desc names a shared owner. Only the unresolved ones are left here.
    if ((strlen prop.vref) > 0) {
      if ((charAt prop.vref 0) == 46) {
        if prop.hasParamDesc {
          def pgPD:RangerAppParamDesc prop.paramDesc
          def pgOwner@(optional):RangerAppClassDesc pgPD.propertyClass
          if (!null? pgOwner) {
            def pgOwnerC:RangerAppClassDesc (unwrap pgOwner)
            if (this.rustClassIsShared(pgOwnerC.name ctx)) {
              pgType = ""
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    def pgState:int 0
    if ((strlen pgType) > 0) {
      if (this.rustClassIsShared(pgType ctx)) {
        if (ctx.in_lhs_of_assignment || rust_recv_place_mut) {
          wr.out(".borrow_mut()" false)
        } {
          wr.out(".borrow()" false)
        }
        pgState = 1
      } {
        pgState = 2
      }
    }
    wr.out("." false)
    def pgSaved:int rust_prop_base_state
    rust_prop_base_state = pgState
    this.WalkNode(prop ctx wr)
    rust_prop_base_state = pgSaved
  }

  ; flattens a string `+` chain into its operand list, left to right, so a
  ; whole chain becomes one format! instead of nested joins and concats
  ; a plain string literal goes into the format string itself; a literal
  ; that holds a brace stays a runtime argument so no escaping is needed
  ; `format!("{}", x.to_string())` prints what `format!("{}", x)` prints —
  ; both are Display — so an explicit to_string of a scalar drops out
  ; writes `"fmt…", arg, arg` — the shared body of println! and format!
  ; does this expression read through an Rc cell rooted at a body local or
  ; parameter? A tail expression's temporaries drop AFTER the block's
  ; locals, so a tail `tok.borrow()…` outlives `tok` (E0597) — such a
  ; return keeps its statement form. A self-rooted read is safe: the
  ; receiver outlives the block.
  ; Pull the INNER self calls of a `self.f(self.g(...))` conflict out to
  ; temporaries, wherever the shape sits in the given tree — a static or
  ; expression wrapper hides it from the direct statement-level path.
  ; Wrapper-aware: the extracted argument may be paren-wrapped, and the
  ; substitution flag goes on the wrapper the arg loops iterate.
  ; Statement level only: this writes `let` statements.
  ; A call made THROUGH one of this object's handle fields —
  ; `langWriter.writeClass(…)` — reads the field out of this object, and the
  ; Ref that read makes lives to the end of the statement, which is the whole
  ; call. The callee reaching back into this object then finds it borrowed.
  ; The handle goes to a local first, so the read ends before the call starts.
  ; walks a function body with the tail statement marked, so the return
  ; custom writes the last `return x;` as the tail expression `x`
  ; a plain scalar path: a name or a field chain with no call, no cell, no
  ; optional and no weak segment — one that reads and writes as plain text
  ; `x = x + e` becomes `x += e` (and -, *, /) for integer and double
  ; targets. Returns true when it wrote the whole statement.
  ; A raw value handed to a parameter that holds a shared class: `(new
  ; VlJson)` is the one shared-class expression that carries no cell of its
  ; own, so the cell goes on at the call site.
  ; The value being stored into a `Rc<RefCell<dyn XTrait>>` field already
  ; holds `Rc<RefCell<Sub>>`, where Sub is a member of X's family. Rust
  ; needs the unsizing coercion, not a second cell — `Rc::new(RefCell::new(
  ; tm.clone()))` is an Rc holding an Rc, which is not the trait object the
  ; field declares. Returns the family root's name, or "".
  fn rustTraitCoerceRoot:string (right:CodeNode fieldTypeName:string ctx:RangerAppWriterContext) {
    if ((strlen fieldTypeName) == 0) {
      return ""
    }
    def tcInner:CodeNode (this.rustUnwrapParens(right))
    def tcT:string (this.rustDeclaredClassOf(tcInner))
    if ((strlen tcT) == 0) {
      return ""
    }
    if (tcT == fieldTypeName) {
      return ""
    }
    if ((this.rustClassIsShared(tcT ctx)) == false) {
      return ""
    }
    ; the value has to BE a handle already; a freshly built object still
    ; needs its cell
    if ((this.rustInitRcState(right ctx)) == 0) {
      return ""
    }
    if ((ctx.isDefinedClass(tcT)) == false) {
      return ""
    }
    def tcCls:RangerAppClassDesc (ctx.findClass(tcT))
    for tcCls.extends_classes tcP:string tcPi {
      if (tcP == fieldTypeName) {
        return fieldTypeName
      }
    }
    return ""
  }

  ; Walk an operand, or the temporary a hoist put its value in. The
  ; hand-written collection operators do not go through the call path, so
  ; they consume the mark themselves.
  ; Can this class derive Clone? A trait-object field cannot, nor a closure
  ; — and neither can a struct that holds one INLINE, however deep: `struct
  ; UIContext { tr : UITextRenderer }` derived Clone while UITextRenderer
  ; could not. A field behind an Rc clones the handle and asks nothing of
  ; what is inside it.
  ; The operators that write one ELEMENT at an index.
  ; Does this expression mention `name` at all — as a bare name or as the
  ; head of a path? `self.ends[self.ends.len() - 1] = i` reads the field it
  ; is writing, and both borrows are live at once.
  ; Does the callee take argument `idx` by `&mut`? Such an argument is an
  ; OUT parameter: it must stay the place it names, because a copy handed to
  ; a `&mut` collects what the callee writes and is then dropped.
  ; A call on THIS object in either of the two shapes the front end builds:
  ; the dotted `this.make()` and the `(call this make args)` an operator
  ; operand carries.
  ; `push this.items (this.make())` — the operator writes through
  ; `&mut self.items` while the call takes `&mut self`, and both borrows are
  ; live in the one statement (E0499). The call goes to a temporary first,
  ; which is what the call path already does for its own arguments.
}

Import "RustOwnership.rgr"
Import "RustCall.rgr"
Import "RustOperators.rgr"
Import "RustClass.rgr"
Import "RustUnion.rgr"
